(function loadMechanicalProperties() {
  'use strict';
  const course = window.MATERIALS_COURSE;
  if (!course) return;
  const pdf = './sources/mechanical-properties.pdf';
  const source = (pages) => `<p class="sourceNote">Lecture: <a href="${pdf}#page=${pages[0]}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mechanical properties part1–3, ${pages.length === 1 ? 'page' : 'pages'} ${pages.join(', ')}</a>.</p>`;
  const figure = (page, alt) => `<figure class="sourceFigure"><a href="${pdf}#page=${page}" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img loading="lazy" src="./assets/mechanical/slide-${String(page).padStart(2,'0')}.webp" alt="${alt}"></a><figcaption>Original lecture, page ${page}. Open the figure to inspect the source page.</figcaption></figure>`;
  const sections = [];
  const add = (eyebrow, title, pages, html, callout) => sections.push({eyebrow,title,sourcePages:pages,html:html+source(pages),callout});
  add('START HERE', 'Choose the property that matches the failure', [1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8], `
    <p>A material can be stiff but brittle, strong but relatively flexible, or ductile but too weak for a given load. Mechanical properties describe different responses to force; one number cannot replace all of them. Start every design or exam problem by asking <strong>what loading occurs, what failure must be prevented, at what temperature, and over what time</strong>.</p>
    <ul class="lessonList"><li><strong>Too much elastic deflection:</strong> examine Young’s modulus and component geometry.</li><li><strong>Permanent bending:</strong> compare stress with yield strength.</li><li><strong>Sudden crack growth:</strong> consider flaws, fracture toughness, temperature, and loading rate.</li><li><strong>Repeated loading:</strong> use fatigue behavior, even when stress is below static yield strength.</li><li><strong>Long exposure at elevated temperature:</strong> check creep, rather than relying on a room-temperature tensile test.</li><li><strong>Surface damage:</strong> consider hardness, wear, contact conditions, and corrosion.</li></ul>
    <p>The Titanic and Challenger images introduce the importance of service conditions. Learn the mechanism—temperature and loading conditions can change failure behavior—rather than treating a historical photograph as a quantitative design rule. Processing also matters: forming, cold working, and heating can alter strength and ductility.</p>
    <div class="conceptCallout"><strong>Study route:</strong> stress and strain → elastic response → yielding and tensile properties → true quantities → bend and hardness tests → impact and cracks → fatigue and creep. Exercise 5 in Exam Practice uses these ideas.</div>`,
    'Strength resists yielding or failure; stiffness resists elastic deformation; ductility measures plastic deformation capacity; toughness measures energy absorption or crack resistance, depending on the test.');

  add('LOAD → STRESS', 'Normalize force and deformation before comparing specimens', [9,11,12], `
    <p>A tensile testing machine pulls a specimen while measuring force and extension over a defined <strong>gauge length</strong>. Force alone depends on specimen size: a thick bar carries more force than a thin bar of the same material. Engineering stress divides the force by the <strong>original</strong> cross-sectional area; engineering strain divides extension by the <strong>original</strong> gauge length.</p>
    <div class="formulaCard"><b>S = F/A₀</b><span>S: engineering stress; F: axial force; A₀: original area. For a round bar, A₀ = πd₀²/4.</span><b>e = ΔL/L₀ = (L − L₀)/L₀</b><span>e is dimensionless. Strain 0.01 = 1%; never insert 1 when you mean 1%.</span></div>
    <p><strong>Tension</strong> pulls apart; <strong>compression</strong> pushes together. Using the usual sign convention, tensile stress/strain are positive and compressive values negative. <strong>Shear</strong> acts tangentially: τ = F/A for a uniform shearing idealization, with shear strain γ approximately the small angular distortion in radians. A uniaxial tensile formula does not automatically describe bending, torsion, or a complicated stress concentration.</p>
    <div class="workedBlock"><b>Worked example — units first</b><span>A 10 mm diameter bar carries 12,000 N. A₀ = π(10)²/4 = 78.54 mm². S = 12,000/78.54 = 152.8 N/mm² = 152.8 MPa. If a 50 mm gauge length extends 0.10 mm, e = 0.10/50 = 0.002 = 0.20%.</span></div>
    <p>Useful conversions: <strong>1 MPa = 1 N/mm²; 1 GPa = 1,000 MPa; 1 psi ≈ 6,894.76 Pa</strong>. Keeping force in N and dimensions in mm makes many exam calculations easier.</p>`,
    'Engineering stress always uses A₀ and engineering strain always uses L₀. Record those original dimensions before loading.');

  add('ELASTICITY', 'Reversible deformation, Hooke’s law, and actual stiffness', [9,10,29], `
    <p><strong>Elastic deformation</strong> is recovered on unloading. In a linear elastic uniaxial region, S = Ee, so the initial slope ΔS/Δe gives <strong>Young’s modulus E</strong>. A larger E means less elastic strain at the same stress. At the atomic scale, elastic strain primarily changes interatomic spacing; the curvature of the bond-energy well helps determine E. It is not the same mechanism as dislocation slip.</p>
    <div class="formulaCard"><b>E = ΔS/Δe; ΔL = FL₀/(A₀E)</b><span>Use the slope of the linear elastic region, not stress divided by total strain after yielding.</span><b>k = F/ΔL = A₀E/L₀</b><span>For a uniform axially loaded bar, component stiffness k depends on material AND geometry.</span><b>τ = Gγ</b><span>Shear modulus G is the slope of the linear elastic shear stress–strain curve.</span></div>
    <p>A long thin steel wire may be much less stiff as a component than a short thick aluminum block despite steel’s higher E. Cold working can raise yield strength substantially without causing a comparable increase in E. Also, <strong>elastic does not necessarily mean linear</strong>: nonlinear elastic materials recover their shape but have a changing tangent slope, as shown in the lecture.</p>
    <div class="workedBlock"><b>Worked example</b><span>With E = 200 GPa = 200,000 MPa and S = 100 MPa, e = 100/200,000 = 0.0005. A 200 mm elastic gauge length extends 0.100 mm. The modulus is 200 GPa, not 200 MPa.</span></div>`,
    'A high modulus does not guarantee a high yield strength. Check separately whether the assumed elastic stress exceeds the material’s yield strength.');

  add('LATERAL STRAIN', 'Poisson’s ratio links axial extension to lateral contraction', [29], `
    <p>When a conventional material is stretched elastically in one direction, it usually contracts in the transverse directions. <strong>Poisson’s ratio ν</strong> measures the negative ratio of transverse strain to axial strain. The minus sign makes ν positive when positive axial extension causes negative lateral contraction.</p>
    <div class="formulaCard"><b>ν = −e<sub>lateral</sub>/e<sub>longitudinal</sub></b><span>For a round bar: e<sub>lateral</sub> = Δd/d₀. In uniaxial linear elasticity, e<sub>lateral</sub> = −νS/E.</span><b>G = E/[2(1 + ν)]</b><span>This relation assumes an isotropic, linear elastic material; it is not a universal relation for every crystal direction.</span></div>
    <div class="workedBlock"><b>Worked example</b><span>E = 70 GPa, ν = 0.33, S = 70 MPa, d₀ = 20 mm. Axial strain = 70/70,000 = 0.001. Lateral strain = −0.33 × 0.001 = −0.00033. Δd = −0.00033 × 20 = −0.0066 mm. The diameter becomes 19.9934 mm.</span></div>
    <p>Do not use the elastic Poisson relation to predict a large plastic reduction in area. Plastic deformation and localized necking require a different description. Nearly incompressible elastic behavior corresponds to ν near 0.5 for an isotropic material, rather than zero lateral strain.</p>`,
    'Keep the sign and the reference dimension: Δd/d₀ is lateral strain; ΔL/L₀ is axial strain.');

  add('READ THE CURVE', 'From elastic loading to yielding, necking, and fracture', [13,14,15,16,27,28,36], `
    <p>Read an engineering tensile stress–strain curve from left to right. First, the material deforms elastically. Beyond yielding, elastic and plastic strains coexist. A ductile metal may then strain harden: a rising flow stress is needed as dislocations interact. The curve reaches its <strong>ultimate tensile strength (UTS)</strong>, the maximum engineering stress F<sub>max</sub>/A₀. In a standard ductile-metal tensile test this approximately marks the onset of localized <strong>necking</strong>.</p>
    <p>After necking, deformation concentrates in a short region and load can fall. Engineering stress falls with that load because its denominator remains A₀; this does not by itself mean the local material has lost all strain-hardening ability. The last point is the engineering <strong>fracture stress</strong> F<sub>fracture</sub>/A₀, which can be lower than UTS. <strong>Uniform elongation</strong> ends at necking; total elongation to fracture includes subsequent localized deformation.</p>
    ${figure(14,'Engineering tensile curve marking initial elastic slope, yield strength, ultimate tensile strength, fracture stress, and strain to failure')}
    <p>The lecture’s metal, thermoplastic, elastomer, and ceramic curves are qualitative. Many ceramics fail in tension with little plastic strain; elastomers can have large nonlinear elastic strains; thermoplastics depend strongly on temperature and time. Do not compare numerical strength simply from sketches with different or unspecified axis scales. Ductile fracture commonly shows necking and a cup-and-cone appearance; brittle fracture shows much less macroscopic plastic deformation.</p>`,
    'Yield strength, ultimate tensile strength, and fracture stress refer to different points. The steepest initial curve is the stiffest, not automatically the strongest or toughest.');

  add('YIELD STRENGTH', 'Construct the 0.2% offset line correctly', [14,15,27], `
    <p>Some materials have clear upper and lower yield points; many do not have a sharp visible transition. The <strong>0.2% offset yield strength</strong> provides a reproducible convention. First find the slope E of the initial linear elastic region. Then start at engineering strain <strong>0.002</strong> on the horizontal axis and draw a line parallel to the elastic line. Its intersection with the measured curve defines the offset yield stress.</p>
    <div class="formulaCard"><b>Offset line: S = E(e − 0.002)</b><span>At the intersection, the modeled elastic strain is S/E and the remaining plastic strain is 0.002.</span><b>e<sub>total</sub> = e<sub>elastic</sub> + e<sub>plastic</sub></b><span>For approximate linear elastic unloading: e<sub>plastic</sub> = e<sub>total</sub> − S/E.</span></div>
    <div class="workedBlock"><b>Worked example</b><span>A point at total strain 0.004 and stress 400 MPa lies on the 0.2% offset line when E = 200,000 MPa: 200,000(0.004 − 0.002) = 400 MPa. Unloading with that slope recovers strain 0.002 and leaves approximately 0.002 permanent strain.</span></div>
    ${figure(27,'Original lecture showing construction of a 0.2 percent offset line and upper and lower yield points')}
    <p>The offset convention is a definition of proof/yield strength, rather than merely a correction for instrument error. Zero-point alignment and measurement accuracy still matter, but they do not explain the physical distinction between elastic and plastic deformation.</p>`,
    '0.2% = 0.002. Do not draw a horizontal line at strain 0.002 or take the stress on the original elastic line at that strain.');

  add('DUCTILITY', 'Measure permanent elongation and reduction of area', [15,16,30], `
    <p><strong>Ductility</strong> is the capacity for plastic deformation before fracture. Two common measures use the broken tensile specimen, with the pieces fitted together to determine the final gauge length L<sub>f</sub>, and the minimum fracture cross-section A<sub>f</sub>. Measurements after unloading exclude the recoverable elastic extension.</p>
    <div class="formulaCard"><b>% elongation = 100(L<sub>f</sub> − L₀)/L₀</b><span>Report the original gauge length when comparing ductility: localized necking makes the percentage gauge-length dependent.</span><b>% reduction in area = 100(A₀ − A<sub>f</sub>)/A₀</b><span>For circular sections, this is 100[1 − (d<sub>f</sub>/d₀)²], not the percentage reduction in diameter.</span></div>
    <div class="workedBlock"><b>Worked example</b><span>L₀ = 50 mm and L<sub>f</sub> = 62 mm give 24% elongation. d₀ = 10 mm and d<sub>f</sub> = 8 mm give 100[1 − 64/100] = 36% reduction in area, although diameter decreased only 20%.</span></div>
    <p>A material can have large elongation yet a low stress level, so ductility alone does not establish high toughness. Conversely, a very strong material that fractures after very little strain can have modest energy absorption. Always compare the property named in the question.</p>`,
    'Ductility is plastic deformation capacity. Elastic stretch and a high modulus do not measure ductility.');

  add('ENERGY', 'Resilience, tensile toughness, and units under the curve', [19,20,30], `
    <p>The area under a stress–strain curve is work per unit original volume in the engineering description. Since strain has no units, the area has stress units: <strong>1 Pa = 1 J/m³</strong> and <strong>1 MPa = 1 MJ/m³</strong>. <strong>Modulus of resilience U<sub>r</sub></strong> is the recoverable elastic energy density up to the elastic limit, commonly approximated using yield strength. <strong>Tensile toughness U<sub>T</sub></strong> includes the entire area to fracture.</p>
    <div class="formulaCard"><b>U<sub>r</sub> ≈ ½S<sub>y</sub>e<sub>y</sub> = S<sub>y</sub>²/(2E)</b><span>Triangular approximation assumes linear elastic behavior to yield.</span><b>U<sub>T</sub> = ∫₀<sup>e<sub>f</sub></sup> S de</b><span>For tabulated points, add trapezoids: ½(Sᵢ + Sᵢ₊₁)(eᵢ₊₁ − eᵢ).</span></div>
    <div class="workedBlock"><b>Worked example</b><span>S<sub>y</sub> = 300 MPa and E = 200,000 MPa give U<sub>r</sub> = 300²/(2 × 200,000) = 0.225 MPa = 0.225 MJ/m³. For a uniform 1 × 10⁻⁵ m³ specimen volume, that corresponds to 2.25 J.</span></div>
    <p>Do not confuse three quantities called toughness in casual wording: tensile curve area (J/m³), Charpy absorbed energy (J), and plane-strain fracture toughness K<sub>IC</sub> (MPa√m). They answer different questions and cannot be substituted by changing a label.</p>`,
    'High toughness generally needs a useful combination of stress capacity and deformation. For a graph question, compare areas, not peak height alone.');

  add('TRUE QUANTITIES', 'Why engineering and true stress separate', [32,33], `
    <p>Engineering stress uses A₀ even as a specimen becomes thinner. <strong>True stress σ = F/A</strong> uses the instantaneous local area. For a uniformly stretching gauge length, <strong>true strain ε = ∫dL/L = ln(L/L₀)</strong> adds successive strain increments relative to the current length. This logarithm makes equal fractional stretches additive.</p>
    <div class="formulaCard"><b>ε = ln(1 + e)</b><span>Applies to a consistently defined uniform gauge-length stretch.</span><b>σ = S(1 + e)</b><span>Uses A₀L₀ ≈ AL: approximately constant volume and uniform deformation. Most useful in uniform plastic tension before necking.</span><b>After necking: σ<sub>local</sub> ≈ F/A<sub>neck</sub></b><span>Measure the local neck area. The stress state is no longer simple uniform uniaxial tension; careful material flow-stress measurements require a necking correction.</span></div>
    <div class="workedBlock"><b>Worked example</b><span>Before necking, S = 300 MPa and e = 0.20. Then ε = ln(1.20) = 0.1823 and σ ≈ 300(1.20) = 360 MPa. Using 20 as the strain would create a physically meaningless answer.</span></div>
    ${figure(33,'Lecture comparison of engineering and true stress–strain curves, with the necking-related interpretation limit')}
    <p>The familiar conversion σ = S(1 + e) is not a general formula for a necked specimen using total gauge extension. After localization, different positions have different strains and areas. At small strain, engineering and true values are close because ln(1 + e) ≈ e.</p>`,
    'State the assumptions before using a conversion. A falling engineering stress after UTS does not prove that the local true stress is falling.');

  add('TEMPERATURE + RATE', 'A test result belongs to its conditions', [8,21,31,45,47,48], `
    <p>Mechanical properties depend on temperature, loading rate, microstructure, and environment. In many metals, increasing temperature reduces yield and tensile strength and increases ductility, while E usually decreases more gradually. These are trends, not universal laws for every material or temperature range. A polymer may change behavior dramatically near its glass transition.</p>
    <p><strong>Strain rate</strong> is strain change per unit time, for example de/dt in s⁻¹. Rapid loading gives some deformation mechanisms less time to operate. Materials can therefore show higher flow stress and reduced impact ductility at high rates. A slowly loaded tensile specimen is not a complete model of a crash, a hammer blow, or long-term service.</p>
    <p>For an exam comparison, hold the other conditions fixed: same material, heat treatment, specimen geometry, and test method. Then explain how the stated change influences the active deformation or fracture mechanisms. An unqualified statement such as “all metals become brittle when cold” goes beyond the lecture evidence.</p>`,
    'Always attach conditions to a property value. Room-temperature strength cannot replace high-temperature creep data or low-temperature impact data.');

  add('BENDING', 'Test brittle materials using a controlled bending geometry', [34,35,36], `
    <p>Brittle specimens are difficult to grip and align in a tensile machine without introducing damaging local stresses. A bend test loads a supported beam, putting one face in tension and the opposite face in compression. Fracture commonly begins at a tensile-side flaw. The <strong>flexural strength</strong>, also called modulus of rupture, is the calculated maximum outer-fiber stress when the specimen breaks; the word “modulus” here does not mean elastic modulus.</p>
    <div class="formulaCard"><b>Three-point rectangular beam: σ<sub>f</sub> = 3F<sub>f</sub>L/(2wh²)</b><span>F<sub>f</sub>: center load at fracture; L: support span; w: width; h: thickness in the bending direction.</span><b>E<sub>bend</sub> = L³m/(4wh³), m = ΔF/Δδ</b><span>Use the initial linear load–deflection slope. Equivalent to FL³/(4wh³δ) only in the linear range.</span></div>
    ${figure(34,'Original three-point bending setup showing the applied load, support span, width, thickness, and deflection')}
    <div class="workedBlock"><b>Worked example</b><span>L = 40 mm, w = 10 mm, h = 4 mm, F<sub>f</sub> = 800 N. σ<sub>f</sub> = 3(800)(40)/[2(10)(4²)] = 300 MPa. If the elastic slope is 2,000 N/mm, E = 40³(2,000)/[4(10)(4³)] = 50,000 MPa = 50 GPa.</span></div>
    <p>These expressions assume the specified three-point, small-deflection beam geometry. In symmetric <strong>four-point bending</strong>, split total applied force F into two loads F/2. If each load is distance L₁ from its nearest support, the maximum moment is <strong>M = FL₁/2</strong> throughout the region between the loads. For a rectangular section, <strong>σ<sub>max</sub> = 6M/(wh²) = 3FL₁/(wh²)</strong>. Three-point bending has its maximum moment only at the center; four-point bending exposes a larger length to maximum tensile stress. Flaw populations, stressed volume, and surface condition can therefore change measured strength. Check whether a problem calls F the total force or the force at each loading point before substituting.</p>`,
    'Use the support span, not total specimen length. Thickness is squared in strength and cubed in the modulus expression, so swapping width and thickness is a serious error.');

  add('HARDNESS', 'Indentation resistance and the test scale', [17,18,37,38,39], `
    <p><strong>Hardness</strong> measures resistance to local indentation or penetration. An indenter is pressed into the surface under a specified loading procedure. It is a convenient local test, but it does not directly measure tensile ductility, bulk toughness, or elastic modulus. Hardness often correlates with strength and wear resistance within a material family; neither correlation is a universal material-independent conversion.</p>
    <div class="compareTable cols3"><div class="compareHead"><span>Test</span><span>Measurement</span><span>Choose / report</span></div><div><span>Brinell</span><span>Ball impression diameter</span><span>Useful for bulk averaging; report ball and load conditions</span></div><div><span>Rockwell</span><span>Depth change under minor/major load sequence</span><span>Report scale, e.g. HRC or HRB; numbers from different scales are not directly comparable</span></div><div><span>Vickers</span><span>Diagonals of a diamond-pyramid impression</span><span>HV; usable across a wide range of hardness and loads</span></div><div><span>Knoop</span><span>Long diagonal of an elongated diamond impression</span><span>HK; useful for small regions and thin/brittle specimens</span></div></div>
    <p>Microhardness uses small loads and impressions to probe individual phases, surface-hardened layers, or local gradients. The lecture gives Vickers loads of roughly 1–100 kgf and micro-Vickers loads of roughly 10 gf–1 kgf as course examples; method selection also depends on specimen thickness, indentation size, spacing, and surface preparation. A hard surface over a soft interior is precisely why a local measurement can differ from a bulk average.</p>
    <div class="formulaCard"><b>HV = 1.8544F/d²; d = (d₁ + d₂)/2</b><span>Vickers: F in kgf, mean of the two impression diagonals d in mm. With F in N use coefficient approximately 0.1891.</span><b>HK = 14.229F/l²</b><span>Knoop: F in kgf, long impression diagonal l in mm. With F in N use approximately 1.451.</span></div>
    <p><strong>Source correction:</strong> slide 18 appears to print 1.72 for the Vickers coefficient. The standard pyramid-geometry coefficient is 1.8544 with force in kgf. Keep the original slide for comparison and use the corrected coefficient with its force convention. <a href="https://empir.npl.co.uk/vamas/wp-content/uploads/sites/60/2020/04/vamas_twa3_report_08.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NPL’s VAMAS hardness guide</a> confirms the Vickers and Knoop coefficients.</p>
    <div class="workedBlock"><b>Worked example</b><span>For a 1 kgf Vickers test with diagonals 0.048 and 0.052 mm, d = 0.050 mm and HV = 1.8544/(0.050²) = 741.8. Averaging diagonal lengths before squaring matches the formula.</span></div>
    ${figure(38,'Original Brinell impression diameter and Rockwell indentation depth diagrams')}`, 
    'A hardness number needs its scale and test conditions. “60 HRC” and “60 HRB” describe different tests.');

  add('BRINELL CALCULATION', 'Keep newtons and kilogram-force conventions consistent', [18,37,38], `
    <p>In the Brinell geometry, D is the ball diameter and d (Dᵢ in the slides) is the measured impression diameter. The denominator below is the curved spherical-cap contact area. A smaller impression at a fixed load indicates greater indentation resistance. The physical restriction is 0 &lt; d &lt; D.</p>
    <div class="formulaCard"><b>HB = 2F/[πD(D − √(D² − d²))]</b><span>In the traditional numerical convention, F is in kgf and diameters are in mm.</span><b>HBW ≈ 0.102 × 2F<sub>N</sub>/[πD(D − √(D² − d²))]</b><span>With force in N, the ≈0.102 factor converts to the conventional hardness-number scale. Using N without it produces a pressure in N/mm², not the same numerical HB value.</span></div>
    <div class="workedBlock"><b>Worked example</b><span>D = 10 mm, d = 4 mm, F = 3,000 kgf. Area = (πD/2)[D − √(D² − d²)] = 13.11 mm². HB ≈ 3,000/13.11 = 228.8. For F = 29,420 N, use the conversion factor and obtain essentially the same conventional number.</span></div>
    <p>Rockwell obtains its number from depth using the chosen scale, so the Brinell diameter equation does not apply to a Rockwell reading. Exact reporting requirements depend on the test procedure; use the units and convention specified in an exam problem.</p>`,
    'The lecture’s Brinell equation needs a force convention. Label it explicitly instead of silently mixing N and kgf.');

  add('NANOINDENTATION', 'A load–depth curve reveals local contact behavior', [40,41,42,43,44], `
    <p>Nanoindentation measures small forces and penetration depths continuously. Hardness follows from maximum load divided by projected contact area; an elastic modulus can be inferred from the <strong>initial unloading stiffness</strong> using a calibrated tip-area function and a contact mechanics model. The inferred indentation/reduced modulus must be converted with the indenter and specimen elastic properties to obtain a specimen Young’s modulus.</p>
    <p>This allows thin films, small phases, and micro- or nanoscale structures to be characterized where a large bulk indentation would obscure the region of interest. The lecture also shows dynamic measurements, scratching/wear, surface mapping, and heated-stage instrumentation. In dynamic testing, phase lag between oscillating load and displacement helps distinguish stored and dissipated mechanical response.</p>
    <p>At small length scales, surface roughness, substrate influence, tip shape, thermal drift, and the chosen analysis model matter. A nanoindentation trace is not interpreted by simply taking load divided by depth. For the exam, know what is measured, which part of the curve informs elastic response, and why small-scale testing is useful.</p>
    ${figure(40,'Lecture nanoindentation diagram illustrating contact depth and the projected contact geometry')}
    <p class="sourceNote">Method clarification: <a href="https://tsapps.nist.gov/publication/get_pdf.cfm?pub_id=907620" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NIST discussion of indentation unloading stiffness and modulus</a>.</p>`,
    'Indentation hardness uses contact area; elastic indentation response uses unloading stiffness. They are extracted from different aspects of the measurement.');

  add('IMPACT', 'Charpy and Izod measure absorbed impact energy', [19,20,45,46], `
    <p>A swinging pendulum strikes and breaks a specimen. The energy lost between the initial and final pendulum heights, corrected for machine losses, estimates absorbed impact energy: <strong>U ≈ mg(h₀ − h<sub>f</sub>)</strong>. The result is usually reported in joules for the specified specimen, notch, temperature, and impact configuration.</p>
    <p><strong>Charpy</strong> uses a horizontally supported beam struck opposite the notch. <strong>Izod</strong> uses a vertically clamped cantilever arrangement. The notch promotes a reproducible stress concentration; support conditions and notch orientation must match the test. High loading rates can reduce the opportunity for plastic relaxation near a notch, so impact behavior can differ from slow tensile behavior.</p>
    ${figure(46,'Lecture Charpy and Izod pendulum impact configurations, specimen supports, and notches')}
    <p><strong>Clarification to slide 45:</strong> a conventional notched Charpy test measures absorbed impact energy; it does not directly return a valid plane-strain K<sub>IC</sub>. A fracture-mechanics specimen and validity criteria are needed for that quantity. The different units are a quick way to catch the confusion.</p>
    <p class="sourceNote">Verified distinction: <a href="https://www.nist.gov/programs-projects/charpy-machine-verification-program" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NIST Charpy test description</a>; <a href="https://store.astm.org/standards/e399" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ASTM E399 fracture-toughness scope</a>.</p>`,
    'Charpy energy is in J. Tensile toughness is in J/m³. KIC is in MPa√m. Keep the three quantities separate.');

  add('DBTT', 'Interpret the ductile-to-brittle transition curve', [2,21,47,48], `
    <p>A <strong>ductile-to-brittle transition</strong> appears when impact energy and fracture character change markedly over a temperature range. At low temperature, some materials absorb little energy and show cleavage-like brittle failure. At higher temperature they permit more plastic deformation and absorb more impact energy. The graph therefore often has a lower shelf, a transition range, and an upper shelf.</p>
    <p>A reported <strong>DBTT</strong> depends on the criterion: a specified impact energy, midpoint between shelves, or a chosen fracture appearance. State the criterion if numerical data allow several interpretations. Ferritic BCC steels commonly show a pronounced transition; FCC metals generally do not show the same sharp cleavage transition in ordinary Charpy testing, as the lecture comparison illustrates.</p>
    ${figure(48,'Original comparison of BCC plain-carbon steel with a pronounced impact-energy transition and FCC stainless steel with no comparable sharp transition')}
    <p>The ship example motivates the need for low-temperature test data. A single universal transition temperature for “steel” is not justified: composition, microstructure, thickness, notch constraint, and strain rate change the response. Do not use the lecture’s historical temperature statement as a property of every steel.</p>`,
    'Below a transition range, a component can become vulnerable to brittle fracture without first undergoing large visible plastic deformation.');

  add('CRACKS', 'Relate fracture toughness to stress and flaw size', [19,22], `
    <p>A sharp crack concentrates stress. In linear elastic fracture mechanics, <strong>stress intensity K<sub>I</sub></strong> describes the severity of the opening-mode crack-tip field; <strong>K<sub>IC</sub></strong> is a critical material resistance measured under valid plane-strain conditions. Y is a dimensionless geometry factor. The definition of a must match the geometry: it may be an edge-crack depth or half the total length of a central crack.</p>
    <div class="formulaCard"><b>K<sub>I</sub> = YS√(πa)</b><span>When K<sub>I</sub> reaches K<sub>IC</sub>, the idealized criterion predicts unstable crack extension.</span><b>S<sub>c</sub> = K<sub>IC</sub>/(Y√(πa)); a<sub>c</sub> = (K<sub>IC</sub>/(YS))²/π</b><span>Use a in m with stress in MPa to obtain K in MPa√m. Yielding or other failure may occur before the predicted fracture stress.</span></div>
    <div class="workedBlock"><b>Worked example</b><span>K<sub>IC</sub> = 40 MPa√m, Y = 1.12, S = 200 MPa. a<sub>c</sub> = [40/(1.12 × 200)]²/π = 0.01015 m = 10.15 mm, using the crack-size convention associated with Y. Doubling stress reduces this idealized critical size by a factor of four.</span></div>
    <p>The plane-strain size check introduced in the lecture has a characteristic minimum scale <strong>2.5(K<sub>IC</sub>/S<sub>y</sub>)²</strong>. It is an inequality/check for sufficient specimen dimensions, rather than an equality defining material thickness. Full validity also considers crack length, ligament, precracking, and test response. A high K<sub>IC</sub> means more crack resistance under the test conditions; it is not itself a measurement of tensile elongation.</p>
    <p class="sourceNote">Scope and validity: <a href="https://store.astm.org/standards/e399" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ASTM E399</a>.</p>`,
    'Put millimeters into meters before taking √a when using MPa√m. Fracture toughness is a resistance; stress intensity is the applied crack-driving quantity.');

  add('FATIGUE', 'Repeated loading can fail a material below its static strength', [23,24], `
    <p><strong>Fatigue</strong> is damage and crack growth under cyclic loading. A crack can initiate at a surface defect, notch, or local slip concentration, then propagate over many cycles before final rapid fracture. Thus a stress below static yield or tensile strength can still cause failure after enough repetitions. Surface condition, inclusions, residual stress, grain structure, temperature, and corrosion all influence life.</p>
    <div class="formulaCard"><b>S<sub>a</sub> = (S<sub>max</sub> − S<sub>min</sub>)/2; S<sub>m</sub> = (S<sub>max</sub> + S<sub>min</sub>)/2</b><span>Amplitude differs from range. Fully reversed cycling has S<sub>m</sub> = 0 and R = S<sub>min</sub>/S<sub>max</sub> = −1.</span><b>S–N curve: stress amplitude versus cycles to failure</b><span>Read the axis definition: some plots use a different stress measure. N is commonly shown on a logarithmic axis.</span></div>
    <p>High-cycle fatigue generally involves predominantly elastic nominal strain and many cycles; low-cycle fatigue involves significant cyclic plastic strain. A <strong>fatigue limit</strong> is an apparent endurance plateau for some material/condition combinations. Many alloys instead have a continuously declining S–N curve, so quote a <strong>fatigue strength at a specified life</strong>. Statistical scatter means one curve is not a guarantee for every specimen.</p>
    <div class="workedBlock"><b>Worked example</b><span>Cycling between +160 MPa and −80 MPa gives S<sub>a</sub> = 120 MPa, S<sub>m</sub> = 40 MPa, and R = −0.5. It is not fully reversed, and its amplitude is not 240 MPa.</span></div>`,
    'A static safety check against yield does not establish infinite fatigue life. Identify amplitude, mean stress, cycle count, and environment.');

  add('CREEP', 'Time-dependent deformation under sustained high-temperature loading', [25,26], `
    <p><strong>Creep</strong> is progressive time-dependent deformation under sustained loading. The lecture emphasizes elevated-temperature metallic service around <strong>T ≳ 0.5T<sub>m</sub></strong>, where both temperatures are absolute kelvin. This is a useful rule of thumb, not a sharp boundary: material, stress, and time matter, and polymers can creep near room temperature.</p>
    <ol class="lessonList"><li><strong>Primary creep:</strong> strain increases but its slope decreases as strain hardening initially dominates.</li><li><strong>Secondary creep:</strong> the slope is approximately constant; hardening and recovery balance. The steady/minimum creep rate is an important design quantity.</li><li><strong>Tertiary creep:</strong> damage, voiding, section reduction, or other instability accelerates deformation toward rupture.</li></ol>
    ${figure(26,'Original strain versus time creep curve showing instantaneous elastic extension and primary, secondary, and tertiary creep')}
    <p>The curve is strain versus <strong>time</strong>, unlike a tensile stress–strain curve. Creep rate is dε/dt, the local slope. In a constant-load experiment, the true stress can rise as the cross-section shrinks; truly constant stress requires load adjustment. That distinction helps explain accelerating deformation.</p>
    <div class="workedBlock"><b>Worked example</b><span>If T<sub>m</sub> = 1,200°C = 1,473 K and service temperature is 500°C = 773 K, T/T<sub>m</sub> = 0.525. Dividing 500 by 1,200 gives the wrong homologous temperature because Celsius has an offset.</span></div>`,
    'Fatigue tracks repeated cycles; creep tracks sustained loading over time. High-temperature components can experience both.');

  add('EXAM METHOD', 'A repeatable workflow for calculations and graph questions', [12,14,27,29,30,32,35,37,49], `
    <p>Before calculating, name the test and the requested property. Write original dimensions and units, calculate area carefully, and distinguish force from stress and extension from strain. Select a formula only after checking its assumptions: elastic versus plastic, uniform versus necked deformation, and three-point versus another bending geometry.</p>
    <ol class="lessonList"><li><strong>List the givens:</strong> F, L₀, ΔL, A₀ or d₀, and any E, ν, or fracture dimensions. Convert units once.</li><li><strong>Identify the region:</strong> E from initial slope; yield from the offset construction; UTS from maximum engineering stress; ductility from fracture dimensions; toughness from area.</li><li><strong>Show substitution:</strong> keep at least one line with numerical values and units, then round only the final result.</li><li><strong>Check scale and meaning:</strong> elastic strain should usually be small; percent and fraction differ by 100; MPa and GPa differ by 1,000.</li><li><strong>Explain why:</strong> if two curves cross, specify whether you compare initial slope, yield stress, peak stress, fracture strain, or total area.</li></ol>
    <p><strong>Quick self-check:</strong> Can you explain why engineering stress can fall while local true stress rises? Why a notched Charpy result is not K<sub>IC</sub>? Why a higher yield strength does not necessarily mean a higher E? Why reduction in diameter differs from reduction in area? Work through Exercise 5 before opening its solutions.</p>
    <p><a href="./sources/mechanical-properties.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open the complete 49-page lecture PDF</a>. Every substantive lecture topic is mapped to a teaching section above; the opening case-study images, instrumentation images, and closing slide are also retained in the original PDF.</p>`,
    'Write the property name, equation, units, assumptions, and interpretation. A correct number without the right physical meaning is fragile exam preparation.');

  const q = (id,prompt,choices,answer,explanation,hint) => ({id,type:'mcq',prompt,choices,answer,explanation,hint:hint || 'Identify the property, the reference dimensions, and the units first.'});
  const drills = [
    q('m05-d01','A 10 mm diameter bar carries 10,000 N. Its engineering stress is approximately:', ['31.8 MPa','127.3 MPa','1,273 MPa','100 MPa'],1,'A₀ = π(10)²/4 = 78.54 mm²; S = 10,000/78.54 = 127.3 N/mm² = 127.3 MPa.'),
    q('m05-d02','A 50 mm gauge length extends 0.25 mm. Engineering strain is:', ['0.5','0.005 (0.5%)','0.05 (5%)','0.0005'],1,'e = 0.25/50 = 0.005; multiply by 100 to express 0.5%.'),
    q('m05-d03','At 100 MPa, a linear elastic material with E = 200 GPa has strain:', ['0.5','0.05','0.0005','0.002'],2,'Convert E to 200,000 MPa, then e = S/E = 100/200,000 = 0.0005.'),
    q('m05-d04','The 0.2% offset line begins at which strain and has which slope?', ['0.2; horizontal','0.002; the initial elastic slope E','0; the plastic slope','0.02; slope 1/E'],1,'0.2% = 0.002. Draw S = E(e − 0.002), parallel to the initial elastic line.'),
    q('m05-d05','The maximum engineering stress is called:', ['Elastic modulus','Fracture toughness','Ultimate tensile strength','Reduction of area'],2,'UTS = Fmax/A₀; it is the peak of the engineering tensile curve, not necessarily its last point.'),
    q('m05-d06','If diameter falls from 10 to 8 mm, reduction in area is:', ['20%','36%','64%','80%'],1,'Circular area is proportional to diameter squared: 100[1 − (8/10)²] = 36%.'),
    q('m05-d07','The area under an engineering stress–strain curve has units:', ['J','MPa√m','J/m³','N/mm'],2,'Stress times dimensionless strain is pressure, and 1 Pa = 1 J/m³.'),
    q('m05-d08','Before necking with nearly constant volume, S = 200 MPa and e = 0.10 imply true stress:', ['180 MPa','220 MPa','200 MPa','2,000 MPa'],1,'σ = S(1+e) = 200(1.10) = 220 MPa. Uniform deformation and approximately constant volume are needed.'),
    q('m05-d09','A conventional Charpy test directly reports:', ['Plane-strain KIC','Elastic modulus','Absorbed impact energy','Fatigue limit'],2,'Charpy measures energy absorbed during the specified impact test, normally in J; a conventional result is not a direct KIC measurement.'),
    q('m05-d10','A larger E by itself implies:', ['Higher yield strength in every case','Less elastic strain at the same uniaxial stress','Greater ductility in every case','Higher Charpy energy in every case'],1,'In linear elasticity e = S/E; increasing E reduces elastic strain at fixed stress. Other properties need separate evidence.'),
    q('m05-d11','With ν = 0.30 and axial elastic strain +0.001, lateral strain is:', ['+0.003','−0.00030','−0.003','+0.00030'],1,'e_lateral = −νe_axial = −0.30 × 0.001 = −0.00030.'),
    q('m05-d12','For three-point bending, doubling specimen thickness at fixed fracture load and span changes calculated flexural stress by:', ['×2','×4','÷2','÷4'],3,'σf = 3FL/(2wh²), so stress varies as 1/h².'),
    q('m05-d13','Which stage has approximately constant creep rate?', ['Primary','Secondary','Tertiary','Instantaneous elastic loading'],1,'Secondary creep has an approximately constant strain–time slope as hardening and recovery balance.'),
    q('m05-d14','For cycling between +120 and −120 MPa, stress amplitude and mean are:', ['240 MPa and 0','120 MPa and 0','0 and 120 MPa','120 and 120 MPa'],1,'Sa = (120 − (−120))/2 = 120 MPa and Sm = (120 + (−120))/2 = 0.'),
    q('m05-d15','Which Brinell statement is correct?', ['Force in N and kgf gives identical numbers without conversion','The diameter equation can be used for HRC','The conventional number requires a consistent force convention','It directly measures tensile elongation'],2,'Use kgf in the traditional numerical equation or the ~0.102 conversion with N. HRC is a Rockwell depth-based scale.'),
    q('m05-d16','Which curve feature identifies a more ductile tensile specimen, all else comparable?', ['A larger fracture strain','A larger initial slope alone','A larger applied force alone','A smaller original gauge length alone'],0,'Plastic deformation capacity is reflected by elongation/reduction of area; compare the same gauge and test conditions.'),
    q('m05-d17','A sharp crack of depth 4 mm is used with stress in MPa and K in MPa√m. Enter a as:', ['4','0.4','0.004','0.000004'],2,'4 mm = 0.004 m. The square-root length in K must be meters for MPa√m.'),
    q('m05-d18','Nanoindentation modulus analysis primarily uses:', ['The specimen color','Initial unloading stiffness and a contact model','Only total specimen length','Charpy pendulum height'],1,'The measured initial unloading slope informs contact stiffness; a calibrated contact area and elasticity model are required.')
  ];
  const tests = [
    q('m05-t01','A 20 mm² bar carries 6,000 N. Engineering stress equals:', ['120 MPa','300 MPa','30 MPa','3,000 MPa'],1,'S = F/A₀ = 6,000/20 = 300 N/mm² = 300 MPa.'),
    q('m05-t02','E = 100 GPa, S = 50 MPa, L₀ = 200 mm, linear elastic loading. Extension is:', ['0.01 mm','1 mm','0.1 mm','10 mm'],2,'e = 50/100,000 = 0.0005 and ΔL = eL₀ = 0.1 mm.'),
    q('m05-t03','A material at total strain 0.006 and stress 400 MPa has E = 200 GPa. Approximate permanent strain after elastic unloading is:', ['0.002','0.004','0.006','0.008'],1,'Elastic strain S/E = 400/200,000 = 0.002. Plastic strain = 0.006 − 0.002 = 0.004.'),
    q('m05-t04','Which statement about yield and ultimate tensile strength is correct?', ['Both must occur at fracture','Yield strength is the curve area','UTS is the highest engineering stress; yield is a separate transition/convention','Yield strength is the initial slope'],2,'Yield marks plastic onset or a specified proof condition; UTS is the maximum F/A₀.'),
    q('m05-t05','A specimen changes from L₀ = 40 mm to Lf = 50 mm after fracture. Elongation is:', ['20%','10%','25%','125%'],2,'100(50 − 40)/40 = 25%.'),
    q('m05-t06','For a linear elastic material, Sy = 200 MPa and E = 100,000 MPa imply approximate resilience:', ['2 MJ/m³','0.2 MJ/m³','20 MJ/m³','0.002 MJ/m³'],1,'Ur = Sy²/(2E) = 40,000/200,000 = 0.2 MPa = 0.2 MJ/m³.'),
    q('m05-t07','With uniform deformation and e = 0.25, true strain is approximately:', ['0.25','1.25','0.2231','0.025'],2,'ε = ln(1.25) = 0.2231.'),
    q('m05-t08','Why can engineering stress fall after necking?', ['Original area increases','Engineering stress uses falling force divided by fixed A₀','Modulus must become zero','True and engineering stress are identical'],1,'Localized necking can reduce carried force, and S retains A₀. Local true stress requires the instantaneous neck area.'),
    q('m05-t09','Which quantity should be compared with MPa√m units?', ['Charpy absorbed energy','Young’s modulus','Plane-strain fracture toughness','Tensile curve area'],2,'KIC carries stress times square-root length. The other quantities have J, MPa, and J/m³ units respectively.'),
    q('m05-t10','If stress doubles in KI = YS√(πa), the predicted critical crack size at fixed KIC and Y:', ['Doubles','Halves','Increases fourfold','Decreases fourfold'],3,'ac = (KIC/(YS))²/π, so ac is proportional to 1/S².'),
    q('m05-t11','The most suitable test for a thin hard surface layer is generally:', ['Only a large bulk tensile specimen','A sufficiently shallow micro/nanoindentation test','Only a pendulum impact test','Only a room-temperature creep test'],1,'A small indentation can localize measurement to the surface layer, with depth and substrate influence controlled.'),
    q('m05-t12','Which is a correct interpretation of DBTT?', ['Every material has the same fixed transition temperature','The reported temperature depends on material, conditions, and chosen criterion','It equals melting temperature','It is obtained from elastic modulus alone'],1,'DBTT describes a transition range; a numerical value needs an energy/fracture-appearance criterion and test conditions.'),
    q('m05-t13','Cycling from 20 to 100 MPa gives amplitude and mean:', ['80 and 60 MPa','40 and 60 MPa','60 and 40 MPa','100 and 20 MPa'],1,'Sa = (100 − 20)/2 = 40 MPa; Sm = (100 + 20)/2 = 60 MPa.'),
    q('m05-t14','A metal melts at 1,000°C and operates at 400°C. Its homologous temperature is about:', ['0.400','0.529','2.500','0.250'],1,'Use kelvin: (400 + 273.15)/(1,000 + 273.15) ≈ 0.529.'),
    q('m05-t15','Which expression uses the correct three-point bending modulus geometry?', ['E = 3FL/(2wh²)','E = FL³/(4wh³δ) in the linear range','E = F/A₀','E = YS√(πa)'],1,'For a small-deflection, rectangular three-point beam, E = L³(ΔF/Δδ)/(4wh³). The other formulas calculate flexural stress, engineering stress, or stress intensity.'),
    q('m05-t16','For a standard engineering tensile graph, the best way to compare tensile toughness is:', ['Compare only the initial slopes','Compare areas up to fracture on common axes','Compare only fracture strains','Compare only peak stresses'],1,'Tensile toughness is the stress–strain integral to fracture, so both stress capacity and strain range matter.'),
    q('m05-t17','A falling creep rate while strain continues to increase identifies:', ['Primary creep','Secondary creep','Tertiary creep','Fatigue limit'],0,'In primary creep the strain–time curve rises with decreasing slope.'),
    q('m05-t18','Which condition alone establishes infinite fatigue life?', ['Stress below static yield strength','A polished surface','A high modulus','None of these alone'],3,'Fatigue depends on cyclic stress, life, mean stress, flaws, environment, material, and statistical reliability. A static check is insufficient.')
  ];
  const m = course.modules.find(x=>x.id==='m05');
  Object.assign(m,{title:'Mechanical Properties',subtitle:'Week 5 · Stress–strain, testing, fracture, fatigue, and creep',description:'Understand every test and curve in the 49-page lecture, with units, assumptions, mechanisms, worked examples, and the original Exercise 5 questions.',available:true,status:'loaded',sourceLabel:'Mechanical properties part1–3 · 49 lecture pages + Exercise 5',sections,drills,testQuestions:tests});
  const refs = [
    ['Engineering stress','S = F/A₀; force divided by original cross-sectional area.','Use N/mm² for MPa.'],
    ['Engineering strain','e = (L − L₀)/L₀; extension divided by original gauge length.','0.002 = 0.2%.'],
    ['Young’s modulus','E is the initial linear elastic stress–strain slope ΔS/Δe.','E describes material stiffness; axial component stiffness is EA₀/L₀.'],
    ['Shear modulus','G is the linear elastic shear slope τ/γ.','For isotropic linear elasticity, G = E/[2(1+ν)].'],
    ['Poisson’s ratio','ν = −e_lateral/e_longitudinal in uniaxial elastic loading.','A positive ν normally means transverse contraction during tension.'],
    ['Offset yield strength','Intersection of the tensile curve with a line of slope E starting at e = 0.002 for 0.2% proof strength.','The offset line is S = E(e − 0.002).'],
    ['Ultimate tensile strength','UTS = maximum tensile force / A₀; maximum engineering stress.','For a standard ductile metal test, approximately the onset of necking.'],
    ['Necking','Localized reduction in cross-sectional area during tensile deformation.','After necking, global engineering-to-true conversions need local information.'],
    ['Ductility','Ability to undergo plastic deformation before fracture.','%EL = 100(Lf − L₀)/L₀; %RA = 100(A₀ − Af)/A₀.'],
    ['Modulus of resilience','Recoverable elastic energy per volume up to the elastic limit.','Ur ≈ Sy²/(2E) for linear elasticity to yield.'],
    ['Tensile toughness','Area under engineering stress–strain curve to fracture.','Units: J/m³; 1 MPa of area equals 1 MJ/m³.'],
    ['True stress','σ = F/A using instantaneous area.','σ = S(1+e) assumes uniform, approximately constant-volume deformation.'],
    ['True strain','ε = ln(L/L₀) = ln(1+e) for uniform gauge stretch.','Logarithmic increments add for successive uniform stretches.'],
    ['Flexural strength','Maximum outer-fiber stress at fracture in a bend test.','Rectangular three-point beam: 3FfL/(2wh²). Also called modulus of rupture.'],
    ['Flexural modulus','Elastic modulus inferred from initial bending load–deflection slope.','Three-point beam: E = L³(ΔF/Δδ)/(4wh³).'],
    ['Hardness','Resistance to localized indentation or penetration.','Report the test scale and conditions; hardness is not a direct toughness measurement.'],
    ['Brinell hardness','Ball-indentation hardness from applied load and impression diameter.','HB = 2F/[πD(D−√(D²−d²))] with F in kgf, diameters in mm; use ~0.102 factor with N.'],
    ['Rockwell hardness','Hardness from depth difference in a prescribed minor/major load sequence.','HRC and HRB are distinct scales.'],
    ['Vickers and Knoop','Diamond-indenter hardness methods useful for small regions.','Vickers uses pyramid diagonals; Knoop uses an elongated impression.'],
    ['Nanoindentation','Instrumented small-scale load–depth measurement.','Contact area gives hardness; initial unloading stiffness helps infer elastic modulus.'],
    ['Charpy impact energy','Energy absorbed in a specified notched pendulum impact test.','Usually J; a conventional Charpy result is not directly KIC.'],
    ['DBTT','Ductile-to-brittle transition temperature under a specified criterion and test condition.','A transition range is common in ferritic BCC steels; ordinary FCC metals generally lack the same sharp transition.'],
    ['Fracture toughness KIC','Critical opening-mode stress intensity under valid plane-strain conditions.','MPa√m; compare with applied KI = YS√(πa).'],
    ['Fatigue','Damage and failure from repeated cyclic loading.','May occur below static yield strength. Sa = (Smax − Smin)/2.'],
    ['S–N curve','Stress measure versus cycles to fatigue failure under specified conditions.','A fatigue limit exists for some systems; otherwise quote fatigue strength at a stated life.'],
    ['Creep','Time-dependent deformation under sustained loading, often important at high homologous temperature.','Primary: rate falls; secondary: nearly constant; tertiary: rate rises. Use kelvin for T/Tm.']
  ];
  refs.forEach((r,i)=>course.reference.push({id:`ref-m05-${String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')}`,term:r[0],definition:r[1],detail:r[2]+' Mechanical properties lecture.',tags:['week 5','mechanical properties',r[0].toLowerCase()]}));
  course.version = 3;
})();
